  <Document>
    <name>💡 LIVE LIGHT POLLUTION</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 6:12AM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 12:12PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE LIGHT POLLUTION</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LATEST AVAILABLE COMPOSITE (annual, hourly check)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/light_pollution/legend.png?v=gibs-black-marble-2016-a85bf00c2e78892d" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Artificial night-sky brightness as a relative index 0-100 (NASA VIIRS Black Marble annual composite, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> near-black pristine dark sky &rarr; navy &rarr; blue rural &rarr; teal fringe &rarr; green suburban &rarr; yellow &rarr; orange urban &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100</b> near-white urban core. Same brightness always shows the same color. Composite visualization index — not raw radiance units; natural sky brightness (moonlight, airglow) is not separated per-pixel, city glow dominates the signal.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NASA VIIRS Black Marble annual nighttime-lights composite via NASA GIBS (Román et al., Suomi NPP VIIRS Day/Night Band)<br/><a href="https://gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov/wmts/epsg4326/best/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml">https://gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov/wmts/epsg4326/best/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE LIGHT POLLUTION</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 6:12AM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 12:12PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE LIGHT POLLUTION</h2><p>Environmental artificial night-sky brightness around the Great Lakes from NASA's VIIRS Black Marble annual composite: a continuous surface showing where city glow and development illuminate the night most strongly and where genuinely dark skies remain. Bright cores mark metropolitan light domes; dark blues mark the darkest shorelines and waters.</p><p>Light-pollution science (a branch of environmental photometry and ecology) studies artificial skyglow from electric lighting: it disrupts circadian rhythms in wildlife and humans, wastes energy, and erases natural starlight. Satellite day/night-band radiometry separates artificial glow from natural night-sky brightness (moonlight, airglow, starlight), mapping an environmental pressure distinct from any single observatory or preserve boundary.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/light_pollution/legend.png?v=gibs-black-marble-2016-a85bf00c2e78892d" width="600" alt="key"><br>Artificial night-sky brightness as a relative index 0-100 (NASA VIIRS Black Marble annual composite, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> near-black pristine dark sky &rarr; navy &rarr; blue rural &rarr; teal fringe &rarr; green suburban &rarr; yellow &rarr; orange urban &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100</b> near-white urban core. Same brightness always shows the same color. Composite visualization index — not raw radiance units; natural sky brightness (moonlight, airglow) is not separated per-pixel, city glow dominates the signal.<br><b>Units:</b> relative brightness index 0-100 (display; linear map of the published composite visualization)<br><b>Source:</b> NASA VIIRS Black Marble annual nighttime-lights composite via NASA GIBS (Román et al., Suomi NPP VIIRS Day/Night Band)<br><b>Update:</b> Composite checked hourly via tile probe; republishes only when NASA publishes a newer annual composite.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov/wmts/epsg4326/best/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml">https://gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov/wmts/epsg4326/best/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml</a></p>]]></description>
